m4_changequote()
kathara-switch(1) -- Manage a Kathara collision domain deployed as a managed switch
=============================================

## SYNOPSIS

`kathara switch` [`-h`] [`-d` <DIRECTORY> \| `-v`]  
<CD_NAME> [<COMMAND> ...]

## DESCRIPTION

Send a command to the management console of the collision domain CD_NAME, which must be deployed in `managed` mode (see `kathara-lab.conf`(5)). Without COMMAND, open an interactive console on the collision domain: each line is a command, and `exit` (or `CTRL+D`) leaves the console.

The collision domain is a VDE switch. The most useful commands are:

* `help`:
    Print the list of the commands.

* `port/print`, `port/allprint`:
    Print the ports which are in use, or all the ports. The interface of a device is described as `kathara <device>:eth<N>`.

* `vlan/print`, `vlan/allprint`:
    Print the VLANs and their ports.

* `vlan/create` <VLAN>, `vlan/remove` <VLAN>:
    Create or remove a VLAN.

* `port/setvlan` <PORT> <VLAN>:
    Set the VLAN of the untagged frames of a port (access port).

* `vlan/addport` <VLAN> <PORT>, `vlan/delport` <VLAN> <PORT>:
    Add or remove a port to the ports which exchange the frames of a VLAN with an 802.1Q tag (trunk port).

* `hash/print`:
    Print the MAC address table.

* `port/sethub` <0|1>:
    Make the collision domain behave as a switch (0) or as a hub (1).

* `fstp/setfstp` <0|1>, `fstp/print`:
    Enable or disable the fast spanning tree protocol, and print its state.

The commands `shutdown`, `load` and `logout` are refused.

The changes made with this command are kept as long as the collision domain is deployed. The port of a device interface keeps its configuration when the device restarts; the VLANs declared in `kathara-lab.conf`(5) are applied when the interface is connected for the first time.

This command is only available with a local Docker daemon on Linux, to the users who are allowed to use it.

## OPTIONS

* `-h`, `--help`:
    Show a help message and exit.

* `-d` <DIRECTORY>, `--directory` <DIRECTORY>:
    Specify the folder containing the network scenario.

    Manage a collision domain belonging to a network scenario that is located inside DIRECTORY.  
	If no `-d` option is provided, assume the network scenario is located in the current directory.
	This option cannot be used in conjuction with `-v` or `--vmachine`.

* `-v`, `--vmachine`:
	The collision domain has been created with vstart command.

	This option cannot be used in conjuction with `-d` or `--directory`.

* <CD_NAME>:
    Name of the managed collision domain.

* <COMMAND>:
    Command sent to the collision domain. If omitted, an interactive console is opened.

## EXIT STATUS

With a COMMAND, the exit status is 0 if the command succeeds and 1 if the collision domain refuses it; the status code and the message of the switch are then printed on the standard error (e.g. `1017 File exists`).

## EXAMPLES

	kathara switch A port/print

Print the ports of the collision domain `A` of the network scenario located in the current folder.

	kathara switch -d path/to/network_scenario A vlan/create 30
	kathara switch -d path/to/network_scenario A port/setvlan 3 30

Create VLAN 30 on the collision domain `A` and make port 3 an access port of this VLAN.

	kathara switch -v A

Open an interactive console on the collision domain `A`, created with `kathara-vstart`(1).

